body:
To make matters worse, luminance in rec709 is non-linear.

From [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rec._709):
> The value is called "luminance" in the standard, and is roughly an approximation of the CIE Y coordinate (which is presumed to measure the perceptual brightness of the color) modified by the non-linear function above. However, since is computed from the non-linear RGB components, this equivalence is correct only for shades of gray. 

So we shouldn't be using any of those coefficients without converting to non-linear sRGB first. We have to make a decision about what color spaces are going in and coming out.

This old [OpenGL tutorial](https://www.opengl.org/archives/resources/code/samples/advanced/advanced97/notes/node140.html) claims to have more accurate coefficients. Based on that I've implemented each function:
```glsl
// Takes a non-linear sRGB color and outputs an approximation of it's luminance.
float luminance_srgb(vec3 rgb) {
	return dot(rgb, vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));
}

// Takes a linear RGB color and outputs an approximation of it's luminance.
float luminance_linear(vec3 rgb) {
	return dot(rgb, vec3(0.3086, 0.6094, 0.0820));
}

// Takes a color and converts it to luminance. The conversion used depends on whether HDR is enabled.
float luminance(vec3 rgb) {
	if (OUTPUT_IS_SRGB) {
		return luminance_srgb(rgb);
	}

	return luminance_linear(rgb);
}
```
A wrinkle with doing this in the engine is that `OUTPUT_IS_SRGB` is not available to CanvasItem shaders. But it shouldn't matter since the code will be generated CPU side.

It also would make sense to exclude `luminance_srgb()` entirely, considering it is a less accurate approximation.
> correct only for shades of gray.

UPDATE:
The above luminance_srgb() is incorrect and should not be used.

As revealed by [a discussion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Luma_(video)) on Wikipedia

> Rec 709 luma simply uses those same numbers, but with the *wrong* order of operations.

These coefficients `0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722` should only ever be used on linear RGB values. The confusion is because 
Rec. 709 used them for both sRGB and linear RGB, which is obviously wrong. Only linear RGB is correct.

It's likely that `0.3086, 0.6094, 0.0820` is wrong/outdated or just not applicable to us.

Therefore, I recommend to use this code instead:
```glsl
// Takes a non-linear sRGB color and outputs an approximation of it's luminance.
float luminance_srgb(vec3 rgb) {
	rgb = srgb_to_linear(rgb);
	return luminance_linear(rgb);
}

// Takes a linear RGB color and outputs an approximation of it's luminance.
float luminance_linear(vec3 rgb) {
	return dot(rgb, vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));
}

// Takes a color and converts it to luminance. The conversion used depends on whether HDR is enabled.
float luminance(vec3 rgb) {
	if (OUTPUT_IS_SRGB) {
		return luminance_srgb(rgb);
	}

	return luminance_linear(rgb);
}
```
